{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"af40798dd2cbcba82","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PCimptx4AK3Hy8xKsVM2q3","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":57433,"tool_uses":22,"duration_ms":149948},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"bd9c6dd8-3896-47fe-a75f-5990f252590f","session_id":"cd38c1e3-03ce-42e8-b8f6-251f05ecd49c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LjPdCR2AEGzrRUn7qJXMnT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016gj2wUfz3WV3xKbTKMfwbP","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"nlp_variance_gtheory","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260520T190053","overall_score":"Weak Accept (4/6)","report":"## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper applies Generalizability Theory to LLM evaluation in a fully-crossed design spanning infrastructure (hardware, precision) x methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) x measurement (item) factors across 6 models, 5 benchmarks, and >2M records. The core finding is that item selection dominates variance (42-58%), direct infrastructure x methodology interactions are negligible (<0.5%), and current evaluation practices yield unacceptably low generalizability (G<0.40). D-study projections provide actionable reproducibility budgets. The methodology is largely sound and the experimental coverage is impressive, but several issues with estimation, design balance, generalizability claims, and missing analyses prevent a higher score.\n\n### Strengths\n\nS1. **Correct and well-motivated G-theory application.** The paper properly applies REML estimation for variance component extraction in a random-effects framework. The choice of G-theory over simpler ANOVA decomposition is well-justified: it enables simultaneous multi-facet decomposition and D-study projections, which are the paper's key practical contribution. The universe score / relative error decomposition for the G coefficient follows standard Brennan (2001) formulations.\n\nS2. **Engine confound identification is a genuine methodological contribution.** The discovery that vLLM routes FP32 through a different compute path than BF16/FP16 (inflating precision variance from 0.3% to 0.87% main, 1.8% to 7.24% interaction) is valuable to the community independent of the G-theory framing. The fix (V0 enforce_eager for all precision modes) is principled and well-documented. This demonstrates the kind of careful confound control that is often missing in LLM evaluation studies.\n\nS3. **Comprehensive experimental coverage.** All 8 planned experiments are reflected in the paper, covering: multi-model (6 models), multi-benchmark (5 benchmarks), multi-hardware (2 GPU types), scale validation (14B), paradigm extension (fine-tuning), and precision mode analysis (LayerCast). The total record count (>2M) provides substantial statistical power. The cross-benchmark gradient (item_id share decreasing with task difficulty) is a genuinely interesting finding.\n\nS4. **Actionable D-study budgets.** The reproducibility budget tables (Tables 7-8) translate abstract variance components into concrete practitioner guidance. The \"10x more effective to triple items than triple prompts\" headline is well-supported by the marginal effectiveness ratios and will be immediately useful to evaluation practitioners.\n\nS5. **Appropriate use of generation-based evaluation.** Following Hua et al. (2025) to avoid log-likelihood scoring artifacts is a sound design choice that strengthens the ecological validity of the findings.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nW1. **The \"fully crossed\" design has important structural gaps.** The paper claims a fully-crossed design but several elements are not fully crossed:\n- Exp-1 (MMLU) uses 3 precisions x 6 prompts x 6 seeds x 3 temperatures x 4 orderings, but Exp-2 (GSM8K/ARC/HellaSwag) uses only 2 precisions x 2 temperatures. This means the cross-benchmark comparisons in Table 3 are not on equal footing -- MMLU has richer factorial coverage and more records (1,036,800 vs 230,400 per benchmark).\n- Hardware is only crossed in Exp-3 (MMLU), not in the multi-benchmark experiments. The separability claim therefore rests on a single benchmark.\n- The 14B extension (Exp-5) uses 2 precisions only, not the full factorial. Claiming \"scale invariance\" from a reduced design on one benchmark is an overstatement.\n- These design imbalances mean the pooled analyses implicitly weight MMLU more heavily. The paper should be transparent about which claims rest on full versus partial factorial evidence.\n\nW2. **200 items is potentially insufficient for stable higher-order interaction estimates.** With 200 items per benchmark and up to 12 variance components (Table 1), some interaction terms (especially 3-way and higher) are estimated from thin margins. The SEs in Table 1 are reported but several CIs are wide relative to the point estimates (e.g., ordering x item: 1.4% with CI [0.8, 2.0]; seed: 0.1% with CI [0.0, 0.2]). The paper does not report whether REML convergence diagnostics were checked, whether negative variance estimates were encountered and how they were handled (boundary constraints or set to zero), or whether bootstrap CIs were used as a robustness check. For a G-theory study, these are standard reporting requirements (Brennan 2001, Chapter 8).\n\nW3. **The 0.5% separability threshold is arbitrary and not well-justified.** The paper defines separability as direct infra x method interactions <0.5% of total variance, and the observed value is exactly 0.5% (Table 5). This is a razor-thin pass. The threshold is not derived from any established criterion in G-theory or psychometrics. Furthermore, \"0.5% of total variance\" is a relative measure that depends on the magnitude of item_id variance -- if items were held fixed (eliminating 56% of variance), the same absolute interaction magnitude would represent ~1.1% of the remaining variance. The paper should either: (a) justify the threshold from first principles or prior literature, (b) report the result on an absolute scale (raw variance component values), or (c) frame it as a continuous quantity rather than a binary pass/fail.\n\nW4. **\"4 models pooled\" analysis conflates model-level and condition-level variance.** Pooling 4 models into a single variance decomposition (Table 1) is problematic because model is not a random facet in the same sense as prompt or seed -- these are 4 specific models, not a random sample from a model population. The pooled analysis implicitly treats model differences as part of the residual or item_id component. The per-model analyses (Table 2) partially address this, but the headline numbers (58.4% item_id) come from the pooled analysis. If model were included as a facet, model x item interactions could absorb substantial variance currently attributed to item_id. The paper should either: (a) include model as a facet and report model x item interactions, or (b) report per-model results as primary and pooled as secondary, with clear caveats.\n\nW5. **D-study projections do not propagate estimation uncertainty.** The D-study budget tables report point estimates of G for each allocation but do not provide confidence intervals on G itself. Since G is a function of estimated variance components, and those components have non-trivial SEs (Table 1), the projected G values inherit estimation uncertainty. A G=0.90 projection could easily be G=[0.85, 0.95] given the component SEs. Without propagated uncertainty, the specific budget recommendations (e.g., \"600 items + 3 prompts + 2 seeds\") appear more precise than warranted. Bootstrap or delta-method CIs on projected G values are standard in D-study practice.\n\nW6. **The fine-tuning extension (RoBERTa-large on 4 NLU tasks) is a weak generalizability probe.** RoBERTa-large is a 355M encoder model fine-tuned on classification tasks -- architecturally and procedurally very different from 7-14B decoder models evaluated via generation. Claiming that \"item dominance extends to fine-tuning\" based on this single architecture on classification tasks is a stretch. The fine-tuning facet structure is also quite different (seed, LR, data shuffle, GPU, precision vs. prompt, seed, temperature, ordering, precision, hardware), making direct comparison of variance shares across paradigms (Table 10) potentially misleading. This extension would be more convincing with at least one generative fine-tuning setting (e.g., SFT of a 7B model).\n\nW7. **Missing dual-metric analysis undermines a key design decision.** The project's decisions.yaml (D002) records a deliberate decision to adopt dual-metric analysis (binary correct + text exact match) as a core methodology, noting that text exact match produces fundamentally different variance structure (prompt x item dominant at 37.5%, G=0.975). This analysis is entirely absent from the paper. Its omission is concerning because: (a) it would substantially complicate the \"item dominance\" narrative (item_id may not dominate under text exact match), and (b) it was identified as a methodological strength. If the dual-metric analysis was dropped, the paper should justify why; if it was never completed, the paper should acknowledge that variance structure is metric-conditional.\n\nW8. **Only 2 GPU types for the hardware facet.** The hardware facet has only 2 levels (RTX PRO 6000 + one other GPU). In G-theory, variance component estimates for facets with very few levels are unstable. With k=2 levels, the hardware main effect and hardware x item interaction have 1 degree of freedom each, providing minimal power to detect non-zero components. The 0.6% hardware main effect could easily be noise. The paper should acknowledge this limitation more prominently rather than drawing strong conclusions about hardware effects from 2 GPUs.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\nQ1. How were negative variance component estimates handled in REML? Were any components estimated as negative and subsequently constrained to zero? If so, which components and in which experiments? This is critical for interpreting the small components (seed: 0.1%, ordering: 0.3%).\n\nQ2. The paper reports 1,036,800 records for Exp-1 as 4 models x 3 precisions x 6 prompts x 6 seeds x 3 temperatures x 4 orderings x 200 items. Was model included as a facet in the REML estimation, or were records from 4 models simply concatenated? If concatenated, model-level variance is confounded with item_id and residual. Please clarify the exact mixed model specification.\n\nQ3. The separability test uses data from Exp-3 (691,200 records on MMLU only). Were separability tests conducted on the other 4 benchmarks? If not, the separability claim is benchmark-conditional, which undermines its generality.\n\nQ4. Table 1 components sum to approximately 95.5% (58.4+16.8+6.1+5.8+2.9+1.8+1.4+0.9+0.7+0.3+0.3+0.1=95.5). What accounts for the remaining ~4.5%? Higher-order interactions? Confounded terms? This should be explained.\n\nQ5. The D002 decision explicitly adopted dual-metric (binary correct + text exact match) analysis as a core methodology, noting that text exact match yields G=0.975 with a completely different variance hierarchy. Why was this analysis excluded from the paper? Does item dominance hold under text exact match?\n\nQ6. For the fine-tuning G-study, was item treated as a random facet in the same way as for inference? In fine-tuning, all training items are used for training, and item_id refers to test items. Is the item_id variance in Table 10 comparable across the two paradigms?\n\nQ7. The paper uses vLLM 0.9.2 with V0 enforce_eager. Were any experiments run with V1 or batch-invariant kernels to quantify how much the engine choice itself contributes to the variance decomposition? Given the engine confound discovery, the variance estimates are conditional on this specific engine configuration.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\nM1. **Table 1 column headers**: \"SE\" and \"95% CI\" are not defined in the table caption. Are SEs computed via REML information matrix, jackknife, or bootstrap? The method matters for interpretation.\n\nM2. **Inconsistent facet levels across experiments**: The method section lists temperatures as \"2-3 levels (T in {0.1, 0.5, 1.0})\" but Exp-2 uses only 2 temperatures. The specific levels used in each experiment should be listed in a supplementary table.\n\nM3. **Table 8 (MATH budget)**: 1,100 items x 3 prompts x 2 seeds = 6,600 evaluations, but the table reports 13,200. This implies either 4 prompts x 3 seeds or some other configuration not stated in the table header (\"3 prompts, 2 seeds\").\n\nM4. **Missing effect sizes**: Variance percentages are relative measures. Raw variance components (on the original score scale) should be reported at least in supplementary material to allow readers to assess practical significance.\n\nM5. **Bibliography incomplete**: Several citations use placeholder information (e.g., \"arXiv:2501.xxxxx\" for ThinkingMachines 2025). Multiple cited papers lack venue, year, or full author information.\n\nM6. **No software/code availability statement.** For a methodology paper advocating specific evaluation practices, releasing the analysis pipeline and variance decomposition code would substantially increase impact and allow independent verification.\n\nM7. **The paper would benefit from a Figure 1** showing the facet structure diagram. The fully-crossed design is the paper's key methodological contribution, and a visual representation would aid comprehension. The current paper has no figures at all.\n\nM8. **Notation**: The G-theory formulation in Eq. 1 uses generic alpha terms but does not specify which terms are estimated in each experiment. A mapping from the generic model to the specific facet structure would help readers unfamiliar with G-theory.\n\n### Scores\n\n- Soundness: 3/5 — Core G-theory application is correct, but the pooled-model analysis strategy, arbitrary separability threshold, missing dual-metric analysis, and lack of uncertainty propagation in D-study projections are non-trivial methodological concerns.\n- Empirical Rigor: 4/5 — Impressive scale and coverage (2M+ records, 6 models, 5 benchmarks), engine confound identification is exemplary, but design imbalances across experiments and the thin hardware facet (k=2) weaken several claims.\n- Overall Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept) — The paper addresses an important gap (unified infrastructure x methodology variance decomposition), provides actionable practical guidance (reproducibility budgets), and the engine confound finding alone justifies publication. However, the methodological concerns (W3-W5, W7) and design imbalances (W1) prevent confident acceptance. Addressing W4 (model as facet), W5 (uncertainty on G projections), and W7 (dual-metric analysis) in revision would elevate this to a clear accept.\n- Confidence: 4/5 — Familiar with G-theory, REML estimation, and LLM evaluation methodology. Reviewed the full paper, experimental design, imagined results, and project context including design decisions."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":745,"cache_read_input_tokens":56129,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":745,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PCimptx4AK3Hy8xKsVM2q3","session_id":"cd38c1e3-03ce-42e8-b8f6-251f05ecd49c","uuid":"121ff953-296d-4273-823c-3e5be22ac74a"}
