{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PCimptx4AK3Hy8xKsVM2q3","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall: Weak Accept (4/6)**\n\nThe paper makes a genuine contribution by unifying infrastructure and methodology variance sources in a single G-theory framework, and the engine confound discovery is valuable independently. The D-study reproducibility budgets are immediately actionable.\n\n**Critical weaknesses identified:**\n\n1. **Design imbalances** -- the \"fully crossed\" claim is overstated. MMLU has richer factorial coverage than other benchmarks; hardware is only crossed on MMLU; the 14B extension uses a reduced design. Claims about separability and scale invariance rest on narrower evidence than presented.\n\n2. **Pooled-model analysis** -- treating 4 specific models as exchangeable observations inflates item_id variance by confounding model x item interactions into the item component. Per-model results should be primary.\n\n3. **Arbitrary separability threshold** -- the 0.5% threshold is unjustified and the observed value (0.5%) lands exactly on the boundary. The result is framed as binary pass/fail when it should be continuous.\n\n4. **No uncertainty propagation in D-study** -- budget recommendations (e.g., \"600 items + 3 prompts + 2 seeds for G>=0.90\") lack confidence intervals, making them appear more precise than warranted.\n\n5. **Missing dual-metric analysis** -- the project's own D002 decision adopted dual-metric analysis as core methodology (text exact match yields G=0.975 with completely different variance hierarchy), but this was dropped from the paper without explanation.\n\n6. **Weak fine-tuning generalization** -- claiming item dominance extends to fine-tuning based solely on RoBERTa-large (355M encoder) on classification tasks is a stretch from the 7-14B decoder inference setting.\n\nThe review report is at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T190053/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: af40798dd2cbcba82 (use SendMessage with to: 'af40798dd2cbcba82' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 61269\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 163399</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd38c1e3-03ce-42e8-b8f6-251f05ecd49c","uuid":"46fd4597-9bca-4afe-babb-8425930adce3","timestamp":"2026-05-20T19:34:41.903Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus areas are **methodology** and **experiments**. Review standard: top ML venue, ~25% acceptance rate, 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: Testing the Separability Assumption Between Infrastructure and Methodological Variance Sources via Generalizability Theory\"\n\nThe paper conducts a fully-crossed Generalizability Theory (G-theory) study spanning infrastructure (hardware type, numerical precision) × methodology (prompt template, random seed, temperature, option ordering) × measurement (test item) factors. Across 4 primary models (7-9B), 2 extension models (14B), 5 benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, MATH), and over 2 million evaluation records, they report three claims:\n\n1. **Item dominance**: Item selection is the single largest variance source (42–58% of total variance), exceeding all other factors combined.\n2. **Separability**: Direct infrastructure × methodology interactions contribute <0.5% of total variance (separable at aggregate), but both factor classes interact substantially with item difficulty (14.2% combined) — conclusions from isolated-factor studies are item-conditional.\n3. **D-study budgets**: Current practice (1 prompt, 1 seed, ≤200 items) yields G < 0.40; tripling items is 10× more effective than tripling prompts; 600 items + 3 prompts + 2 seeds → G ≥ 0.90 on MMLU at 18× cost.\n\nAdditionally: fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa-large on 4 NLU tasks (item_id still rank-1 at 37–45%), 14B model extension confirming scale invariance, engine confound discovery (vLLM FP32 routing through different compute path), and LayerCast precision analysis.\n\n## Key Tables and Data\n\n**Table 1** (MMLU variance decomposition, 4 models pooled, 1,036,800 records):\n- item_id: 58.4% (SE 2.1, CI [54.3, 62.5])\n- residual: 16.8%\n- prompt×item: 6.1%, temp×item: 5.8%, seed×item: 2.9%, precision×item: 1.8%, ordering×item: 1.4%\n- Main effects: prompt 0.9%, temperature 0.7%, precision 0.3%, ordering 0.3%, seed 0.1%\n\n**Table 2** (Per-model item_id on MMLU): Qwen3-8B 59.8%, Llama-3.1-8B 57.1%, Gemma-2-9B-IT 62.0%, Mistral-7B-v0.3 54.7%\n\n**Table 3** (Cross-benchmark): MMLU 58.4%, ARC 55.1%, HellaSwag 52.7%, GSM8K 48.7%, MATH 42.1%. G(current): 0.24–0.36.\n\n**Table 5** (Separability test, 691,200 records): Direct Infra×Method: 0.5%; Infra×Item: 3.6%; Method×Item: 15.5%; Item Main: 56.1%\n\n**Table 7** (D-study budget MMLU): 200/1/1→G=0.34; 600/3/2→G=0.90; 1200/4/3→G=0.95\n\n**Table 8** (Cross-benchmark G≥0.90 budget): HellaSwag 550 items, MMLU 600, ARC 650, GSM8K 800, MATH 1100\n\n**Table 9** (14B models): Qwen3-14B item_id 57.4%, DS-R1-14B 53.8% — within 8B range\n\n**Table 10** (Inference vs Fine-tuning): Both item_id rank-1; fine-tuning has seed (8.7%) and lr (6.3%) as major secondary factors\n\n**Table 11** (Precision modes): All within 0.5pp accuracy; LayerCast item agreement with FP32 = 0.999\n\n**Engine confound**: Preliminary FP32 showed 8.5pp gap vs BF16 (precision main 0.87%, precision×item 7.24%). After V0 enforce_eager fix: gap → 0.5pp, precision main → 0.3%, precision×item → 1.8%.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full experiment plan includes 8 experiments:\n- exp-001: Full-scale precision study (MMLU, 4 models, 1,036,800 records) ✓ appears covered\n- exp-002: Multi-benchmark replication (GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag) ✓ appears covered\n- exp-003: Multi-hardware G-study (2 GPU types, 691,200 records) ✓ appears covered\n- exp-004: D-study projections ✓ appears covered\n- exp-005: 14B model extension ✓ appears covered\n- exp-006: Fine-tuning G-study (RoBERTa-large) ✓ appears covered\n- exp-007: LayerCast precision study ✓ appears covered\n- exp-008: MATH benchmark extension ✓ appears covered\n\nAll 8 imagined experiments appear to be reflected in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions. Be rigorous but fair. For each, give specific evidence from the paper.\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n- Is the G-theory framework correctly applied? Is the REML estimation appropriate?\n- Is the fully-crossed design actually fully crossed, or are there unbalanced/nested elements?\n- Is the separability test well-defined (the 0.5% threshold)?\n- Is the confound control (V0 enforce_eager) methodologically sound?\n- Are the variance components estimated with adequate precision (check SEs and CIs)?\n\n### 2. Experimental Design\n- Are the facet levels sufficient? (6 prompts, 6 seeds, 2-3 temperatures, 4 orderings, 200 items, 2 GPUs, 4 precisions)\n- Is 200 items per benchmark sufficient for stable variance component estimates?\n- Is the model selection representative? (4 primary 7-9B models, 2 extension 14B)\n- Is stratified sampling of items appropriate?\n\n### 3. Statistical Rigor\n- Are variance percentages meaningful without raw effect sizes?\n- Is the G coefficient derivation standard G-theory?\n- Do the D-study projections account for estimation uncertainty in the variance components?\n- Is \"4 models pooled\" the right analysis strategy vs per-model analysis?\n\n### 4. Experimental Completeness & Coverage\n- Does the paper cover all planned experiments?\n- Are there missing analyses that would strengthen the claims?\n- Is the fine-tuning extension (RoBERTa-large) adequate for the claim generality?\n\n### 5. Weaknesses\n- What are the key methodological or experimental weaknesses?\n- What critical experiments or analyses are missing?\n\n### 6. Minor Issues\n- Any presentation issues in methods/results?\n- Notation consistency?\n\n## Output Format\n\nStructure your review as:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\n...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. ...\nW2. ...\n...\n\n### Questions for Authors\nQ1. ...\nQ2. ...\n...\n\n### Minor Issues\nM1. ...\nM2. ...\n...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Empirical Rigor: X/5\n- Overall Score: X/6\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"af40798dd2cbcba82","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall: Weak Accept (4/6)**\n\nThe paper makes a genuine contribution by unifying infrastructure and methodology variance sources in a single G-theory framework, and the engine confound discovery is valuable independently. The D-study reproducibility budgets are immediately actionable.\n\n**Critical weaknesses identified:**\n\n1. **Design imbalances** -- the \"fully crossed\" claim is overstated. MMLU has richer factorial coverage than other benchmarks; hardware is only crossed on MMLU; the 14B extension uses a reduced design. Claims about separability and scale invariance rest on narrower evidence than presented.\n\n2. **Pooled-model analysis** -- treating 4 specific models as exchangeable observations inflates item_id variance by confounding model x item interactions into the item component. Per-model results should be primary.\n\n3. **Arbitrary separability threshold** -- the 0.5% threshold is unjustified and the observed value (0.5%) lands exactly on the boundary. The result is framed as binary pass/fail when it should be continuous.\n\n4. **No uncertainty propagation in D-study** -- budget recommendations (e.g., \"600 items + 3 prompts + 2 seeds for G>=0.90\") lack confidence intervals, making them appear more precise than warranted.\n\n5. **Missing dual-metric analysis** -- the project's own D002 decision adopted dual-metric analysis as core methodology (text exact match yields G=0.975 with completely different variance hierarchy), but this was dropped from the paper without explanation.\n\n6. **Weak fine-tuning generalization** -- claiming item dominance extends to fine-tuning based solely on RoBERTa-large (355M encoder) on classification tasks is a stretch from the 7-14B decoder inference setting.\n\nThe review report is at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T190053/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":163399,"totalTokens":61269,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3919,"cache_read_input_tokens":56874,"output_tokens":475,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3919},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":475,"cache_read_input_tokens":56874,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3919,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3919,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":12,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":5}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ac371550dd0c1db93","tool_use_id":"toolu_01NbsNFhNkibC7PwKidrNJaJ","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":65073,"tool_uses":24,"duration_ms":141918},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"0fdcbb70-199a-45de-8645-4a3b77fb6ca5","session_id":"cd38c1e3-03ce-42e8-b8f6-251f05ecd49c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EcPv93cQnhoD7sM9KYERUA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017aY8KY3VGWcEJZnXFceivF","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation, crossing infrastructure factors (hardware, precision) with methodology factors (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) and item selection in a single factorial design. The main findings are that item selection dominates variance (42-58%), infrastructure and methodology are separable at the aggregate level but interact through item difficulty, and D-study projections show current practices are severely underpowered (G < 0.40). The work directly extends Mesting et al. (2026), who applied G-theory to methodological facets only, by adding infrastructure facets and testing separability. While the experimental scale is impressive (2M+ records, 5 benchmarks, 6 models), the novelty is incremental over Mesting, the item dominance finding is largely predictable from psychometric first principles, and the related work has significant gaps.\n\n### Strengths\n\nS1. **Well-motivated separability test with nuanced outcome.** The distinction between aggregate separability (<0.5% direct interactions) and item-conditional non-separability (14.2% combined) is genuinely useful. This is the paper's strongest conceptual contribution — it provides a principled answer to whether isolated-factor studies are valid, with appropriate caveats. The two-level framing (aggregate license + item-conditional warning) is more informative than a binary separability verdict.\n\nS2. **Scale and breadth of the experimental design.** Crossing 7 facets across 6 models, 5 benchmarks, and 2M+ records is a substantial empirical effort. The cross-benchmark gradient (item dominance decreasing with task difficulty, from 58.4% on MMLU to 42.1% on MATH) is a non-obvious finding that adds genuine insight about why reasoning benchmarks produce less reproducible results.\n\nS3. **Engine confound discovery.** The identification that vLLM routes FP32 through a different compute path than BF16/FP16, inflating precision variance from 0.3% to 0.87% (and precision x item from 1.8% to 7.24%), is a concrete methodological contribution of independent value. This is actionable for anyone doing precision comparisons and illustrates how infrastructure confounds can systematically bias variance attribution.\n\nS4. **D-study budgets are actionable.** The 10:1 marginal effectiveness ratio (items vs. prompts) and specific budget recommendations (600 items + 3 prompts + 2 seeds for G >= 0.90) translate directly into evaluation design guidance. The cross-benchmark budget table (550-1100 items depending on task) adds practical value.\n\nS5. **Fine-tuning extension broadens scope.** Showing that item dominance persists in fine-tuning (37-45%) while the secondary variance hierarchy shifts (seed/LR replace prompt/temperature) demonstrates the framework's generality beyond inference evaluation.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nW1. **Incremental novelty over Mesting et al. (2026).** The paper's core methodological contribution — applying G-theory to LLM evaluation — is Mesting's. What this paper adds is (a) infrastructure facets and (b) the separability test. But infrastructure turns out to be nearly negligible: precision contributes 0.3% main effect and hardware 0.6%. The separability test yields a largely expected null result at the aggregate level (it would be surprising if GPU type x prompt template interacted strongly). The item-conditional nuance is more interesting but is a secondary finding. The paper risks appearing as \"Mesting + two more facets that turn out not to matter much.\"\n\nW2. **Item dominance is predictable from psychometric first principles.** In any assessment with heterogeneous item difficulty, item variance will dominate when other factors have small effects. MMLU items range from trivial recall to expert reasoning — of course this produces massive item variance. The paper's own Discussion section essentially explains why this must be the case (\"steep difficulty gradient within every benchmark\"). The 58% figure quantifies what psychometricians would predict qualitatively. The paper does not adequately engage with whether this finding is genuinely surprising vs. a confirmation of well-known measurement theory. The counter-argument in the paper framing doc (\"the magnitude was not previously quantified\") is weak — the magnitude is a function of the specific item difficulty distribution in these benchmarks, not a universal property of LLM evaluation.\n\nW3. **Significant related work gaps.** Several important bodies of work are missing:\n\n- **Classical G-theory beyond Cronbach 1972.** Brennan (2001, \"Generalizability Theory\") is the standard modern reference and is entirely absent. Shavelson & Webb (1991) is missing. The median paper draft cites Brennan but the best-case paper does not. This is a serious omission for a paper whose primary methodological framework is G-theory.\n\n- **Reproducibility in ML broadly.** Pineau et al. (2021, \"Improving Reproducibility in ML: A Report from the NeurIPS Reproducibility Program\") and the ML reproducibility checklist literature are not cited. Bouthillier et al. (2021, \"Accounting for Variance in Machine Learning Benchmarks\") directly decomposed hyperparameter vs. seed vs. dataset variance using ANOVA — this is the closest ML-native predecessor to the present work and is completely missing.\n\n- **IRT-based item analysis in NLP.** Item Response Theory is the dominant psychometric framework for item analysis, and there is growing work applying IRT to NLP benchmarks (e.g., Lalor et al. 2019; Rodriguez et al. 2021 on IRT for NLP evaluation; Vania et al. 2021). The paper's finding that item selection dominates would be enriched by connecting to IRT-based difficulty estimation.\n\n- **Prompt ordering sensitivity.** Lu et al. (2022, \"Fantastically Ordered Prompts and Where to Find Them\") is a seminal work on ordering effects in LLM evaluation. The paper includes ordering as a facet but does not cite this foundational work.\n\n- **Benchmark contamination and item difficulty.** The item dominance finding intersects with work on benchmark contamination (Jacovi et al. 2023; Deng et al. 2024) and adaptive/dynamic benchmarking, which are not discussed.\n\n- **Variance decomposition in deep learning.** Beyond Bouthillier, there is work by Henderson et al. (2018, \"Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters\") on seed sensitivity and Dodge et al. (2020) on fine-tuning instability, both performing variance decomposition without G-theory.\n\nW4. **Positioning vs. Mesting is incomplete.** The paper claims to \"extend\" Mesting by adding infrastructure facets, but does not adequately address key differences: (a) Mesting included judge as a facet (43.8% variance), which this paper deliberately excludes — this makes the variance structures incomparable, and the paper's item dominance finding might not hold if judge were included; (b) Mesting studied API models with opaque infrastructure, while this paper studies open-weight models — the papers study fundamentally different evaluation settings; (c) the paper does not discuss whether its variance estimates are consistent with Mesting's for the overlapping facets (prompt, seed, temperature, item). A direct numerical comparison on shared facets would strengthen the positioning.\n\nW5. **D-study budgets are a straightforward computation, not a conceptual contribution.** Once variance components are estimated, D-study projections are a mechanical application of G-theory formulas (plug in different facet levels, compute G). Calling this a \"practical tool\" contribution overstates it — it is the standard output of any G-study. The novelty is in the variance component estimates, not in the D-study arithmetic.\n\nW6. **The \"first\" claims are overclaimed.** The paper claims \"first fully-crossed G-theory study spanning infrastructure x methodology x measurement.\" But Mesting (2026) already conducted a G-study of LLM evaluation with multiple crossed facets including items. The incremental \"first\" is adding hardware and precision facets to an existing G-theory framework. Similarly, \"first formal test of separability\" is a stretch — any multi-factor ANOVA implicitly tests interactions, which is the same as separability. The paper should frame its contribution as \"extending\" rather than claiming multiple \"firsts.\"\n\nW7. **Model as a fixed effect raises questions.** The paper pools across 4 models but treats model as fixed (not a random facet). This means the variance decomposition is conditional on this specific model set. If model were treated as a random facet (as in Mesting), model x item interactions might absorb a substantial portion of what is currently attributed to item main effect. The paper does not discuss this design choice or its implications.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\nQ1. How would the variance decomposition change if model were treated as a random facet rather than fixed? Specifically, what fraction of the current \"item_id\" variance would be absorbed by model x item interactions? This is critical because the item dominance claim (58.4%) may be inflated by pooling across models with different difficulty profiles.\n\nQ2. Can you provide a direct numerical comparison with Mesting et al. (2026) on overlapping facets? Specifically, what are Mesting's estimates for item, prompt, temperature, and seed variance, and how do yours compare? If they differ substantially, what explains the discrepancy (different models, different evaluation protocol, different item samples)?\n\nQ3. If judge were added as a facet (as in Mesting, where it contributed 43.8%), would item dominance still hold? Your design decision to fix the judge is defensible but means your variance structure is not directly comparable to the most closely related prior work.\n\nQ4. The 0.5% threshold for separability appears arbitrary. What is the justification for this specific threshold? Would the conclusions change at 1%? At 2%? The multi-hardware G-study reports exactly 0.5% for direct interactions — right at the threshold — which warrants sensitivity analysis.\n\nQ5. The paper treats \"item selection\" as a single construct, but items vary in multiple dimensions (difficulty, domain, format, discrimination). Can you decompose item variance further — e.g., into difficulty, domain, and residual item components? This would make the \"prioritize item count\" recommendation more actionable (should practitioners sample more items within domains, or across domains?).\n\nQ6. Bouthillier et al. (2021) conducted ANOVA-based variance decomposition across datasets, hyperparameters, and seeds for ML benchmarks generally. How do your findings compare? Their work predates yours and covers similar ground using simpler methods. What does G-theory add beyond what ANOVA decomposition already provides?\n\n### Minor Issues\n\nM1. The bibliography has only 7 entries for a paper of this scope. A related work section citing only 7 papers at a top venue will raise immediate red flags. Expect 30-50 references minimum.\n\nM2. The title is 22 words long and contains a colon, a subtitle, and a methodological term. Consider shortening — e.g., \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation via Generalizability Theory.\"\n\nM3. The abstract mentions \"over 2 million evaluation records\" but the experiments sum to approximately 2.7M records. Be precise.\n\nM4. The term \"empirical license\" for isolated-factor studies is loaded — it suggests this paper has authority to grant permission to other researchers. Consider \"empirical support for\" instead.\n\nM5. Table 1 reports residual at 16.8% — this is a large unexplained component. The paper should discuss what residual captures (higher-order interactions, measurement error, model-level stochasticity beyond seed).\n\nM6. The paper uses \"item dominance\" as a finding label, but in G-theory the term \"facet\" is standard. \"Item\" is the object of measurement, not a facet in the traditional sense. The paper should clarify the terminological mapping from psychometrics to ML evaluation more carefully.\n\nM7. The fine-tuning G-study uses RoBERTa-large, a 355M encoder model, which is architecturally very different from the 7-14B decoder models in the inference experiments. The generalizability of fine-tuning findings to modern LLM fine-tuning (e.g., LoRA on 7B+ models) is questionable.\n\nM8. Cronbach et al. (1972) citation is to the book \"The Dependability of Behavioral Measurements\" but the correct G-theory foundational reference is Cronbach, Gleser, Nanda & Rajaratnam (1972), which the paper does cite correctly in the bib — but should verify the title matches.\n\n### Scores\n\n- Novelty: 2.5/5 — The infrastructure extension over Mesting is modest (infrastructure contributes negligibly), the item dominance finding is predictable, and the D-study budgets are mechanical. The separability test and engine confound discovery provide genuine but limited novelty.\n- Significance: 3/5 — The practical recommendations are useful and the scale is impressive, but the core message (\"use more items\") is not paradigm-shifting. The 18x cost factor for G >= 0.90 may actually discourage adoption.\n- Related Work Completeness: 2/5 — Major gaps in classical G-theory references, ML reproducibility literature, IRT, and variance decomposition predecessors. Only 7 citations is far below venue expectations.\n- Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) — A competent empirical study with impressive scale, but incremental novelty over Mesting et al. (2026), predictable main finding, significant related work gaps, and overclaimed \"first\" contributions. The separability test and engine confound are the strongest novel elements but are secondary to the paper's framing. With substantially expanded related work, more careful positioning (emphasizing extension rather than \"firsts\"), and deeper engagement with why item dominance is or is not surprising, this could reach borderline accept.\n- Confidence: 4/5 — I am familiar with G-theory, the LLM evaluation reproducibility literature, and the ML benchmarking literature. I have read the cited papers. My uncertainty is primarily around whether the engine confound finding would be valued more highly by systems-oriented reviewers.\n","project":"nlp_variance_gtheory","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260520T190053","overall_score":"Weak Reject (3/6)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":747,"cache_read_input_tokens":63716,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":747,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NbsNFhNkibC7PwKidrNJaJ","session_id":"cd38c1e3-03ce-42e8-b8f6-251f05ecd49c","uuid":"92922189-fd85-4e9b-bd50-0073fd9b0f4c"}
